DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'plots.pl', 'PGgraphmacros.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

# make sure we're in the context we want
Context("Numeric");

do {
($w1, $h1) = (random(3,6), random(3,6));
($w2, $h2) = (random(2,4), random(5,7));
} until ($w1*$h1-$w2*$h2 > 0); 

$graph = Plot(
    xmin => 0, 
    xmax => 16,
    xvisible => 0,
    xtick_delta => 5,
    xminor => 4,
    ymin => 0,
    ymax => 10,
    ytick_delta => 2,
    yminor => 1,
    yvisible => 0,
    aspect_ratio => 1
);

$graph->add_rectangle([1,1], [$w1+1,$h1+1], fill_color => 'blue', fill => 'self');
$graph->add_rectangle([8,2], [8+$w2,2+$h2], fill_color => 'darkorange', fill => 'self');
$graph->add_label(1+0.5*$w1,1+$h1,'Room 1', v_align => 'bottom');
$graph->add_label(8+0.5*$w2,2, 'Room 2', v_align => 'top');


BEGIN_PGML
Room 1 and Room 2 are each shaped like a rectangle.  
A model of each room is shown below and each square is one yard on each side. 

>>[!!]{$graph}{500}<<

How much larger is room A than room B?  [_]{Real($w1*$h1-$w2*$h2)} square yards
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
You could do this two different ways.

One way is to just count the orange ([$w1*$h1]) and blue ([$w2*$h2]) rectangles and
subtract to get [$w1*$h1-$w2*$h2] yards.

Another way is to notice that Room 1 is [`[$w1]\times [$h1]=[$w1*$h1]`] square yards and
Room 2 is [`[$w2]\times [$h2]=[$w2*$h2]`] square yards and then subtract to get the same result. 
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();